description.narrative:
- The Q&A booklet on C.105 and C.29 was developed and disseminated by the MOLISA as part of efforts for public awareness-raising and capacity building on the elimination of forced labour. As result, the National Assembly overwhelmingly voted for the ratification of C105 in June 2020. The C105 enters into force from 14 July 2021  (7/8 core conventions);
- As follow up, in 2021, the Government issued their implementation plan for the application of C.105 ineffective coordination among tripartite partners and other stakeholders; 
-   The Government with its high commitment, has established the technical advisory group for studying the possibilities for ratification of C.131 on Minimum Wage. The draft dossier is available for submission for appraisal before the end of 2021.
- Strongly commitment from Viet Nam Government has been made through agreeing (Memo of Understanding) in promoting the ratification and implementation of ILS in Viet Nam as well as ensuring the participation of representatives of workers and employers in the entire process. - In close collaboration with the Government and the EU delegation in Viet Nam, the ILO through an EU funded project has provided effective technical and advocacy support to influence key decision-makers (not only MOLISA but also Ministry of Justice, Ministry of Foreign Affair, President Office, National Assembly, and social partners) for their support of ratification of C105. 
- Regarding the process for C.131 ratification, in 2020 and early 2021, the ILO has supported MOLISA and tripartite partners to develop 2 reports on the topic. The remaining work for proceeding with the ratification  is expected to be done in late 2021 and the beginning of 2022;
- Under the persistent and continuous advocacy work of the ILO Viet Nam a Memorandum of Understanding to push the promotion of international labour standards in Viet Nam over the next 10 years has been signed between ILO and MOLISA on 20 May 2021 in Hanoi.  The signed MoU has included the plan for ratification of 15 ILO conventions by 2030 including C.87 on Freedom Association and protection of the right to organize.  
- For the promotion of the ILS application, the flyer Convention and Frequently Asked Questions on ILS (including in the Covid-19 situation) have been developed and published by the ILO.
- First-ever ILS stock-taking report was published and distributed national wide by the ILO.  The report set out the context for engaging the tripartite constituents; the Government of Viet Nam, employers and workers organizations; and development partners and stakeholders in deciding decent work priorities that are well informed and anchored in the use and application of international standards.
